- PR #387 (Draft PR-A: domain-aware legal source routing) is not part of this release.
- PR #252 (Simplify Secure Case Workspace access on latest main) is not part of this release.
- These open PRs must be treated as future-version work and must not be assumed to be live.
- GitHub Actions emitted a Node.js 20 deprecation warning for upstream action packages; it did not block or invalidate the successful production deployment.
- The current evidence pack does not record the previous stable Cloudflare Worker version ID. Before any manual rollback, identify the target stable version in Cloudflare Workers > Deployments or via Wrangler deployment/version listing.

## 7. Rollback / recovery
For this bounded runtime release, use a Cloudflare Worker version rollback rather than the broader production-runtime-recovery workflow.

Cloudflare documents that a Worker can be rolled back to a previously deployed version from the dashboard or with `wrangler rollback`. A rollback creates a new deployment pointing to the selected prior Worker version; connected resources are not themselves changed by the rollback. Compatibility with any changed external resource must still be checked before rollback.

Do not use the broader `production-runtime-recovery.yml` as the default rollback for this release, because that workflow includes migration and secret-restoration operations beyond the bounded runtime release scope.

